WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:06.428
My name's Kevin Harrison,

00:00:06.428 --> 00:00:08.794
and I'm the owner and
operator of Project Laser,

00:00:08.794 --> 00:00:10.680
which is a mobile laser-cleaning service

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:11.823
in Western Australia.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:15.452
Lasers are really good at cleaning soot

00:00:15.452 --> 00:00:17.880
off basically any hard substrate.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.279
So that can be a wooden beam,

00:00:20.279 --> 00:00:22.654
a steel beam, a painted wall,

00:00:22.654 --> 00:00:26.130
paving stones, bricks,
all sorts of surfaces,

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.650
whether it's really smooth or porous.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.170
We're sending thousands of
light pulses every second

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:32.850
into the surface contamination.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.130
This is getting absorbed into the soot,

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:36.530
and it's basically vaporizing it

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:38.271
or popping it off the surface.

00:00:39.842 --> 00:00:42.360
On this day, we were called in to help out

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:43.830
remove smoke damage from a home

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.990
that had a Molotov
cocktail thrown into it.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.842
There was smoke filled throughout the home

00:00:48.842 --> 00:00:49.733
on both stories.

00:00:49.733 --> 00:00:52.016
Like, really, really dark black soot,

00:00:52.016 --> 00:00:53.520
and we had to remove smoke damage

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:54.900
from every single surface.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.625
So this was within the kitchen,

00:00:56.625 --> 00:00:58.710
lounges, bathrooms.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.930
We used our large laser for this job,

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.670
because it's got the 15-meter hose.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:05.100
We parked the vehicle
right next to the house

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.290
and then just extended
the fiber-optic cable

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.570
through a door that had been kicked in.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.330
And that gave us access to
the majority of the house.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:15.090
This was a really long process.

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:17.760
Basically worked from left to right.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.693
Once we finished laser cleaning,

00:01:19.693 --> 00:01:21.576
we did wipe down the walls just to ensure

00:01:21.576 --> 00:01:24.960
there's no vagrant soot on the surface.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.210
We use a pulse laser,
which is basically a tool

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:30.030
to remove thin layers of
surface contamination.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:31.772
So, the benefits of a pulse laser,

00:01:31.772 --> 00:01:33.330
it has no abrasive power.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.100
So no matter how hard we try,

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.860
we can't profile, scratch,
or damage a surface.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.230
Porous brick is going to
absorb more of the soot.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:41.805
It's got all the cracks and crevices

00:01:41.805 --> 00:01:44.071
where soot will sit, and you may have to

00:01:44.071 --> 00:01:45.660
clean at certain different angles,

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:46.702
whereas if you've got a nice,

00:01:46.702 --> 00:01:48.110
smooth, painted surface,

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:49.770
it's all even, there's no soot

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.960
hiding in different cracks and crevices.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:53.760
Here we're doing smoke-damage removal

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:56.726
with our small 200-watt mobile laser

00:01:56.726 --> 00:01:58.626
on a rammed-earth wall.

00:01:58.626 --> 00:02:00.570
If you'd run your fingers over the wall,

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.250
it was all crumbly and falling apart.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:05.010
So if they'd chosen to
use high-pressure water,

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:06.540
chemicals, and scrubbing brushes,

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.520
they would've removed lots of material.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.136
This was a bush fire that basically

00:02:10.136 --> 00:02:12.269
came up to the home and licked the wall

00:02:12.269 --> 00:02:14.340
and then caught the trusses on fire.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.560
So there was also some
smoke-damage cleaning

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.705
inside the house that we had to carry out.

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:21.630
When you're using a
laser-cleaning machine,

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:23.790
you need to take safety very seriously.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.430
It is very easy to mitigate the risks.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:27.600
Firstly, that can be done

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.580
by wearing the correct safety glasses.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.710
So for us, that's wearing safety glasses

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:33.018
that protect your eyes from the

00:02:33.018 --> 00:02:35.676
1,064 infrared laser pulses.

00:02:35.676 --> 00:02:37.500
Secondly, we've spent

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.050
an uncomfortable amount
of money on respirators.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:42.480
So everyone who operates the laser

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.610
will be wearing a respirator.

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:45.685
The beautiful thing about

00:02:45.685 --> 00:02:47.068
using a laser-cleaning machine

00:02:47.068 --> 00:02:48.780
is you can really adjust the parameters

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:50.520
depending on what you're cleaning.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.530
If you want to clean
a larger area quicker,

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:53.970
you can have a beam that goes,

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:56.419
for example, 100 millimeters wide,

00:02:56.419 --> 00:02:58.202
or about 3½ inches.

00:02:58.202 --> 00:03:00.030
If you wanted to do large areas,

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:02.073
you could turn the power
up and have a large beam.

00:03:02.073 --> 00:03:02.998
And at the same time,

00:03:02.998 --> 00:03:04.648
you could really wind the power down

00:03:04.648 --> 00:03:07.750
if you wanted to remove
soot off a chandelier

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:10.140
or a bathtub or the side of a TV.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.210
You really could wind it down enough

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:15.660
to not damage even plastics, if necessary.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:17.351
With our smaller laser,

00:03:17.351 --> 00:03:19.801
it's got a really defined focal zone,

00:03:19.801 --> 00:03:21.290
an inch or a couple centimeters.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:23.880
So if you're not perfectly
within that focal zone,

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.710
the laser's not going to
be effectively cleaning.

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:27.330
The beauty of the laser

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:29.910
is you can just move
backwards and forwards slowly,

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:32.340
and you'll hear when the
laser is at its loudest,

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.530
which is basically when it's
chewing through the soot.

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:36.330
You could laser clean
with your eyes closed

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.455
and just use your ears

00:03:37.455 --> 00:03:39.021
once you get used to the sound.

00:03:39.021 --> 00:03:41.280
On this job, we're using our 500-watt

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.130
laser-cleaning machine
to remove smoke damage

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:46.200
from a pool pump that caught on fire.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:47.500
Once we arrived on site,

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:48.733
we saw that we could easily put

00:03:48.733 --> 00:03:51.240
our fiber-optic cable over the fence.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.120
Unfortunately, there was a
lot of heat with this fire,

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.340
so there was a bit of
area where the bricks

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:58.110
were permanently oxidized.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:03:59.640
So there was a fair few spots

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:02.040
where there's actually
permanent damage on the bricks.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:05.070
We took off all the
old plug points, screws

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:06.210
that were still stuck in the wall.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.320
This just enabled us

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.570
to really clean the whole wall properly.

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:11.820
If we need to protect any hoses or cables

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:12.803
when we're laser cleaning,

00:04:12.803 --> 00:04:15.462
put blue masking tape over the surface,

00:04:15.462 --> 00:04:18.450
or ideally you put aluminium-foil tape.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:22.140
So aluminium reflects up
to 95% of infrared light.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:25.440
So it's really easy to
stop the laser pulses.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.060
And laser cleaning is very different

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.460
to traditional cleaning
methods for removing soot.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.040
Externally, they would
use high-pressure water.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.230
Internally, on painted surfaces,

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:36.720
you're generally using a degreaser,

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:40.050
a bleach, countless rags, chemical gloves.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:42.330
You'd be spraying the
chemical onto the surface,

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:43.505
be wiping it off,

00:04:43.505 --> 00:04:45.930
and then you'd be using more cleaning rags

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:48.030
just until that surface was clean enough

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:50.460
that they could apply
another paint over the top.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.680
Lasers are basically a single pass.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:55.470
There's no water, there's
no chemicals getting used.

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:57.390
It's definitely the cleanest process

00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:00.060
both internally and externally.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.280
Laser cleaning's a brilliant technology.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:03.810
It's really only getting started,

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:05.910
in the sense of being mobile.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.220
So the technology is
really growing rapidly.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:10.650
Every city in the world
is, in the next few years,

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.030
going to have a laser-cleaning company

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.250
that specifically targets smoke damage.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:15.253
It's a good feeling to be

00:05:15.253 --> 00:05:17.003
one of the first companies doing it

00:05:17.003 --> 00:05:18.503
and doing it well.

